WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.107
A door should stay locked unless you

2
00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:08.958
have a card and the right code.

3
00:00:08.958 --> 00:00:12.700
So the program tests: not, card and code.

4
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.592
A programmer tidies it up: not card

5
00:00:16.592 --> 00:00:17.969
and not code.

6
00:00:17.969 --> 00:00:21.905
Four cases are possible: card and code

7
00:00:21.905 --> 00:00:25.250
card only, code only, or neither.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.463
Is the rewrite the same test?

9
00:00:28.463 --> 00:00:30.240
Pause and decide.

10
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.993
To answer

11
00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:39.801
we need an arithmetic of true and false.

12
00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:44.141
Boolean algebra has just two values

13
00:00:44.141 --> 00:00:45.196
T and F.

14
00:00:45.196 --> 00:00:50.474
Two variables give four rows: T T, T F, F T

15
00:00:50.474 --> 00:00:51.060
F F.

16
00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:51.786
And

17
00:00:51.786 --> 00:00:55.635
x and y is true only when both are true.

18
00:00:55.635 --> 00:00:58.264
That is the first row only.

19
00:00:58.264 --> 00:00:58.639
Or

20
00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:02.583
x or y is true when at least one is true

21
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:04.930
including when both are.

22
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.054
Not flips a value: not T is F

23
00:01:10.054 --> 00:01:12.600
and not F is T.

24
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:14.744
Now x implies y.

25
00:01:14.744 --> 00:01:17.873
It is false in exactly one row

26
00:01:17.873 --> 00:01:19.829
x true and y false.

27
00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:23.642
That is the broken case from part one.

28
00:01:23.642 --> 00:01:27.260
In the other three rows, it is true.

29
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.803
Why true when x is false?

30
00:01:29.803 --> 00:01:31.557
Think of a promise

31
00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:35.329
if you score ninety or more, you get an A.

32
00:01:35.416 --> 00:01:37.872
A student who scores seventy

33
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.556
cannot break it, whatever grade they get.

34
00:01:41.556 --> 00:01:44.626
So the rows with x false are true.

35
00:01:44.626 --> 00:01:47.170
We call them vacuously true.

36
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.940
And x if and only if y is true exactly

37
00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.000
when x and y have the same value

38
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.520
the first row and the last.

39
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.284
Two expressions are equal when their

40
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.119
truth tables agree in every row.

41
00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:07.095
That gives laws.

42
00:02:07.095 --> 00:02:11.395
Commutative: x and y equals y and x.

43
00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:16.625
Distributive: x and, y or z, equals x and y

44
00:02:16.625 --> 00:02:18.020
or x and z.

45
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:22.525
Double negative: not not x is x.

46
00:02:22.525 --> 00:02:26.810
And x and not x is always false.

47
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.257
Each law is proved by its truth table.

48
00:02:30.257 --> 00:02:32.621
One variable gives two rows.

49
00:02:32.621 --> 00:02:34.740
Two variables, four rows.

50
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.104
Three variables, eight rows.

51
00:02:37.104 --> 00:02:40.365
Checking all eight is a complete proof

52
00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:42.484
unlike the whole numbers

53
00:02:42.484 --> 00:02:44.440
no cases are left over.

54
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:46.598
Back to the door.

55
00:02:46.598 --> 00:02:49.383
Column three is not, x and y.

56
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:52.168
It is false in the first row

57
00:02:52.168 --> 00:02:54.860
and true in the other three.

58
00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.796
Column four is not x, or not y.

59
00:02:58.796 --> 00:03:03.076
Row by row: false, true, true, true.

60
00:03:03.076 --> 00:03:07.240
The two columns agree in every row

61
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.438
so they are equal.

62
00:03:09.438 --> 00:03:12.330
That is De Morgan's law.

63
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.803
Now the programmer's rewrite

64
00:03:15.803 --> 00:03:17.634
not x and not y.

65
00:03:17.634 --> 00:03:20.542
False, false, false, true.

66
00:03:20.542 --> 00:03:24.203
It differs in rows two and three.

67
00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:26.895
With a card but no code

68
00:03:26.895 --> 00:03:30.880
the real test keeps the door locked

69
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.680
but the rewrite opens it.

70
00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:38.244
Not does not simply pass through and.

71
00:03:38.244 --> 00:03:40.750
It flips and into or.

72
00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:44.452
Truth tables also settle part one.

73
00:03:44.452 --> 00:03:48.583
Here is x implies y, and its contrapositive

74
00:03:48.583 --> 00:03:50.511
not y implies not x.

75
00:03:50.511 --> 00:03:52.807
They agree in every row

76
00:03:52.807 --> 00:03:55.010
so they are equivalent.

77
00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.477
The converse, y implies x

78
00:03:57.477 --> 00:04:00.001
differs in rows two and three.

79
00:04:00.082 --> 00:04:02.606
So a statement and its converse

80
00:04:02.687 --> 00:04:04.560
are not the same thing.

81
00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.777
Last, every expression falls into

82
00:04:08.887 --> 00:04:11.201
one of three classes.

83
00:04:11.201 --> 00:04:14.949
A tautology is true in every row

84
00:04:14.949 --> 00:04:16.822
like x or not x.

85
00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:21.120
A contradiction is false in every row

86
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.104
like x and not x.

87
00:04:23.214 --> 00:04:26.410
A contingency is true in some

88
00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.275
rows and false in others

89
00:04:29.275 --> 00:04:31.700
like x implies not x.

90
00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:37.035
Here is a useful tautology: if x implies y

91
00:04:37.035 --> 00:04:40.628
and x is true, then y is true.

92
00:04:40.628 --> 00:04:45.033
Every direct proof uses it: assume A

93
00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:49.090
know that A implies B, conclude B.

94
00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:50.532
To sum up.

95
00:04:50.618 --> 00:04:54.410
Two Boolean expressions are equal when their

96
00:04:54.496 --> 00:04:57.254
truth tables agree in every row.

97
00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.581
With m variables there are

98
00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:01.218
two to the m rows

99
00:05:01.218 --> 00:05:04.149
and checking them all is a proof.

100
00:05:04.149 --> 00:05:06.476
And not flips and into or

101
00:05:06.476 --> 00:05:08.630
that is De Morgan's law.

102
00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:13.267
Try it yourself: make the truth table of

103
00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:15.147
x or y, implies x.

104
00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.709
Is it a tautology, a contradiction

105
00:05:18.709 --> 00:05:20.490
or a contingency?

106
00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.452
Next lecture: lists, sets

107
00:05:23.452 --> 00:05:25.360
and counting them.
